-- What splitting a signature's loan groups rejects - Analysis-Borrows.md §4.2 and §8.2.
-- Each function is one rule.

data Cell {value: Int}

-- A result in a group no parameter is in. The old count - "is anything marked" - passes, because
-- both parameters carry a marker; what it cannot see is that neither carries *this* one, so the
-- result is a loan of nothing.
fn emptyGroup(a: l'Cell, b: r'Cell) -> x'Cell = a

-- The same with one parameter, which is the shape a typo takes: the group on the result is not the
-- group on the parameter, and nothing else in the signature says so.
fn mistyped(a: src'Cell) -> dst'Cell = a

-- A loan group written in an alias. §4.3 gives a `data` declaration a slot to bind a label to; an
-- alias has none, so a label here would be numbered in the alias's own environment and collide with
-- the groups of whichever signature used it.
alias Loose = src'Cell

-- Resolved on use, so the alias needs one. The two errors after the group's are the cascade: with
-- the group refused the result is an ordinary `'Cell`, which nothing in the signature roots.
fn usesLoose(a: Cell) -> Loose = a

-- A record *is* allowed to name its field origins - that is §4.3, and `LoanSlot.yana` is where it
-- is exercised. What is still refused is a use that does not bind the slot: the field's group is
-- numbered in the record's own environment, so a signature reading it without a binding would be
-- reading an index that means something else there.
data Cursor(a) {at: src'Cell, tag: a}

fn unbound(c: Cursor(Int)) -> src'Cell = c.at

-- The group stated twice. `return` means the signature's one anonymous group and the tick on the
-- type names a group of its own, so one of the two would have to win silently. The bare `'` marker
-- that used to be writable here is gone: the name area holds the binding convention and the name
-- and nothing else, so that argument patterns can have it.
fn statedTwice(return a: src'Cell) -> src'Cell = a

-- (`&a: src'Cell` used to be refused here as a capability written twice. It is now the canonical
-- spelling - the sigil carries the capability and the tick carries the group, each axis written
-- once - and it means the same declaration as `a: &src'Cell`, since an exclusive reference in a
-- parameter's type folds into the convention. See LoanGroup.yana.)

-- A result reference that names no group, where the signature has more than one. `-> &Cell` is a
-- complete contract while there is one relationship to be in - it is what the whole library writes
-- - and says nothing at all once there are two. §4.8 rule 5's safe form: where the count is not
-- one the rule does not apply and the signature is rejected, because the alternative is picking.
fn underSpecified(a: &l'Cell, b: &r'Cell) -> &Cell = a

-- And the loan the groups keep apart is still a loan: `keeps` says the result is rooted in `a`, so
-- writing through `a` while the result lives is two writers of one place, exactly as it is when
-- there is one group. Splitting says which arguments are free, not that any of them are.
fn keeps(a: &l'Cell, b: &r'Cell) -> &l'Cell = a

fn writesThroughRoot() -> Int:
    let &first = Cell {value: 1}
    let &second = Cell {value: 2}
    let &picked = keeps(first, second)

    first.value = 5
    return picked.value
